<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maison Carrée</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maison Carrée is a Roman temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nîmes</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, built under Augustus and dedicated around the turn of the era to his grandsons and adopted heirs Gaius and Lucius Caesar. It is one of the best-preserved Roman temples anywhere, a model of classical architecture that inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a>, and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maison-carree.jpg" alt="Maison Carrée" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Krzysztof Golik · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maison_Carree_in_Nimes_(16).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Maison carrée, French for square house, is an ancient Roman temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nîmes</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and one of the best-preserved Roman temples to survive in the territory of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. It is a mid-sized Augustan provincial temple of the imperial cult, a caesareum. The temple dominated the forum of the Roman city. It stands on a podium 2.85 m high and measures 26.42 m by 13.54 m, a rectangle almost twice as long as it is wide. Its deep portico, almost a third of the building's length, emphasises the temple front and distinguishes the layout from Greek temples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The temple was dedicated or rededicated to Gaius and Lucius Caesar, grandsons and adopted heirs of Augustus who both died young. The dedicatory inscription was removed in medieval times, but a local scholar, Jean-François Séguier, reconstructed it in 1758 from the order and number of the holes in the frieze and architrave to which bronze letters had been fixed. In his reconstruction it reads: to Gaius Caesar, son of Augustus, consul; to Lucius Caesar, son of Augustus, consul designate; to the princes of youth.</p><p>The temple is similar to the Tuscan-style Roman temple described by the contemporary writer Vitruvius, though it uses the Corinthian order. It is hexastyle, with six Corinthian columns under the pediment at each end, and pseudoperipteral, with twenty engaged columns along the walls of the cella. A large door leads to the small windowless interior, which has no ancient decoration. The carving is not as precise as that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Until the nineteenth century the temple formed part of a larger complex of adjoining buildings, which were demolished when it housed, from 1821 to 1907, what is now the Musée des Beaux-Arts de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nîmes</a>. The present door dates from 1824, and a restoration between 1988 and 1992 re-roofed it and cleared the square, revealing the outlines of the forum. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> had a stucco model made of the temple in 1785 while minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and it inspired the Virginia State Capitol he designed, as well as the Église de la Madeleine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and St Marcellinus Church in Rogalin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. It was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in September 2023.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maison carrée” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nemausus became a Latin colony with rich monuments, including the <b>Maison Carrée</b> and the aqueduct carried by the Pont du Gard.</blockquote><a href="/state/volcae-arecomici">Volcae Arecomici</a></li></ul></section>
